/-!
# The twisted global sections of a coherent sheaf on `Proj` of a Noetherian graded ring

Let `S = ⨁_{n ≥ 0} 𝒮 n` be a Noetherian graded ring, `X = Proj S` and `ℱ` a coherent
`𝒪_X`-module. This file proves part (2) of [Stacks, Tag 0B5R], equivalently clause (4) of
[Stacks, Tag 0B5Q]: for every `k ∈ ℤ` the graded `S`-module

`⨁_{n ≥ k} Γ(X, ℱ(n))`

is a finite `S`-module. No generation hypothesis on `S` appears: in particular `S` is not
assumed generated in degree one over `S₀`, and it is exactly the absence of that hypothesis that
the source's Veronese reduction is for.

## The source's proof

[Stacks, Tag 0AG6] proves the same statement for a graded ring generated by finitely many
elements of degree one, and that is `Stacks.ProjCoherentDegreeOne.moduleFinite_truncation`. For a
suitable `d` the Veronese subring `S⁽ᵈ⁾` *is* generated in degree one over its degree-zero part,
and `X' = Proj S⁽ᵈ⁾` is isomorphic to `X` by [Stacks, Tag 0B5J]. So the degree-one theorem can be
run on `X'`, provided the degrees are split into the `d` residue classes: writing `n = md + q`
with `0 ≤ q < d`,

`ℱ(md + q) = (ℱ(q))(md)`  and  `⨁_m Γ(X, (ℱ(q))(md)) ≅ ⨁_m Γ(X', (i_*(ℱ(q)))(m))`,

the second an isomorphism of graded `S⁽ᵈ⁾`-modules. The right-hand side is a finite
`S⁽ᵈ⁾`-module by [Stacks, Tag 0AG6] (4) applied on `X'` to the coherent module `i_*(ℱ(q))`.
There are finitely many residue classes, and `S` is a finite `S⁽ᵈ⁾`-module, so the whole
truncation is a finite `S`-module.

The degree `d` carrying all the properties the argument uses at once is
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjCoherentII.exists_reductionDegree`. What is built here is the
semilinear bijection of one residue class,

`Γ(X', (i_*(ℱ(q)))(m)) ≅ Γ(X, ℱ(md + q))`,

assembled from the comparison of [Stacks, Tag 0B5J] on twisted sections, the twist-of-a-twist
isomorphism `(ℱ(q))(dm) ≅ ℱ(dm + q)` of [Stacks, Tag 03GJ], and the degree bookkeeping that
reconciles their index conventions.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjCoherentII.veroneseSectionsComparison`: the comparison
  `Γ(X', ℱ'(m)) ≅ Γ(X, 𝒢(dm))` for a sheaf `𝒢` on `X` identified with the pullback of `ℱ'`.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjCoherentII.residueSectionsComparison`: the comparison
  `Γ(X', ℱ'(m)) ≅ Γ(X, ℱ(md + q))` obtained from it when `𝒢 = ℱ(q)`, by a twist of a twist.

## Main results

* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjCoherentII`
  `.veroneseSectionsComparison_homogeneousScalarTwistedSections` and
  `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjCoherentII`
  `.residueSectionsComparison_homogeneousScalarTwistedSections`: the two comparisons are
  semilinear over the inclusion `S⁽ᵈ⁾ ⊆ S`.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjCoherentII.moduleFinite_truncation_of_isCoherent`:
  `⨁_{n ≥ k} Γ(X, ℱ(n))` is a finite `S`-module.

The three bijections being composed disagree about which side of the product the degree `d` is
written on: `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseTwistedSectionsAddEquiv` lands in degree `m · d`, while
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ModuleTwistIso.twistSheafIterateSections` is indexed by `d · m`, and
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjTwistedSections.exists_finite_span_residue` asks for `m · d + q`.
The two degree equations reconciling them are `veroneseIterateDegree` and `iterateResidueDegree`.

The lower bound upstairs is `k' = ⌊(k - q - 1)/d⌋ + 1`, and `residueBoundIff` is the equivalence
`k' ≤ m ↔ k ≤ md + q` that `exists_finite_span_residue` asks for. It is an equivalence and not an
implication: the transport needs both that the image of the truncation upstairs lands in the
truncation downstairs, and that every degree of the residue class in the truncation downstairs is
hit from above.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B5R](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B5R)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B5Q](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B5Q)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0AG6](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AG6)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B5J](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B5J)
-/

